      "Window": {
        "properties": {
          "anchor": {
            "type": "string",
            "const": "index",
            "title": "Anchor",
            "default": "index"
          },
          "from": {
            "type": "integer",
            "title": "From"
          },
          "to": {
            "type": "integer",
            "title": "To"
          },
          "unit": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "days",
              "months",
              "years"
            ],
            "title": "Unit"
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "type": "object",
        "required": [
          "from",
          "to",
          "unit"
        ],
        "title": "Window",
        "description": "A time window counted in whole units from the index date.\n\nUnit 0 is the index day itself. Positive units count forward from the day\nafter it and negative units count back from the day before it. Both\n`from` and `to` are inclusive, so:\n    from=-365, to=-1,  unit=days    the 365 days before the index date\n    from=1,    to=365, unit=days    the 365 days after it\n    from=-12,  to=-1,  unit=months  the 12 months before it\n    from=1,    to=12,  unit=months  the 12 months after it, from the next day\nFor days this simply means day k is the index date + k.\n\nThe index day belongs to neither side. An ED visit on the day of\nenrolment may be the reason for the enrolment, so it is neither a clean\nbaseline event nor an outcome of the programme.\n\nA source that records dates only by month or year cannot say where in\nthat month or year an event fell. On such a source the index date and\nthe event dates are both cut to the source's resolution before the\nwindow is applied, so unit 0 is the whole calendar month or year that\ncontains the index date, and unit k is the one k steps from it. On the\nyear-granularity condition table, from=-5, to=-1 is the five calendar\nyears before the index year. Plain date arithmetic there would let events\nfrom later in the index month or year into a window that is meant to end\nbefore the index date."
      }
